#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101673} :activity/id #uuid "6d230526-c4b2-501d-9f6c-bd0d45bc17a8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102027 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 (x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$ Can we solve it using the method of integration by substitution?\n\nThe answer is yes, and the procedure is very similar to the one that we followed for the indefinite integrals. The main difference is that this time, we need to change the limits of integration too. We proceed via three steps, as follows.\n\n **Step 1**: Change of variable in the integrand. \n\nWe start by setting the base of the power equal to $u.$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x=u^5\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\n **Step 2**: Calculate the new limits. \n\nWe now have to change the limits of integration, $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Since we want to express the integral in terms of the new variable, $u,$ we need to change the variable in the limits too.\n\nThe lower limit $x=1$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =1-1 \\\\ u & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile the upper limit $x=2$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =(2)-1 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is useful to use a table like the following when changing limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Solve the integral in the new variable $u,$ using the new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}(0)^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when applying integration by substitution to definite integrals, there is no need to substitute the variable $x$ back in the final answer. We can simply evaluate it using the new variable $u.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f735e38-f75f-56a8-a1cb-955ca2d3bfd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 840 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "27565782-e098-5a61-a8c2-27791d647aea" :step/math-academy-id 1618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100704} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dcde3b5-5cdf-5266-9392-0835053a7ded" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050771}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650336}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of Function Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "eb61b7f0-eeed-5bf1-a132-36d2ff2abc72" :step/math-academy-id 1619 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100705} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d63e99a-9db4-565e-97c5-ffc3090b0074" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650349}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "d4b6cefe-e2af-5b96-87b0-359eae941e3d" :step/math-academy-id 1623}] :activity/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101028}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/id #uuid "95c6ac75-ad8e-5eab-9a14-9a2372fa3af7" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100704 :question/id #uuid "2359455c-4a44-5794-8b86-738bcdbbd11d" :question/math-academy-id "e-455" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^2+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}{|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}-{2}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-16}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x\n$$ became $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u.\n$$ When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x.$ Same for the upper limit."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dcde3b5-5cdf-5266-9392-0835053a7ded" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of Function Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100705 :question/id #uuid "dd931400-d592-5402-b380-dee7812adac5" :question/math-academy-id "e-456" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^3+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d63e99a-9db4-565e-97c5-ffc3090b0074" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"}]}
